{"parentUuid":"f8dfaff3-6ccf-4931-be96-afd496975660","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"4e89693d-4ff6-4bfd-965d-9b7c2be0b420","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/zenray/.claude/skills/think-of-everything\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nSurface the FEW risks that matter on ANY decision — code, business, life, art — each proven against the real facts, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. This pass answers the question asked, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against reality instead of memory.\n\n**Specialist rule (anti-generic), applies to every output line:** write at the level of the expert who gets paid to review exactly this — immigration lawyer, senior rigger, tax advisor, DB architect. Every kept risk must contain at least one exact value from the real context (number, date, name, path, price, spec) verified this session. A risk that could be pasted unchanged into someone else's situation is generic: sharpen it or cut it.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (two lines)\n\n- Domain + the specialist to embody + type: (a) action to take, (b) existing thing to audit, (c) pure decision, nothing built yet.\n- For (b), the source of truth is the actual artifact (`git diff`, the contract, the scene file), never its description.\n- Trivial, cheap and reversible case: say so, jump to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is for anything expensive, irreversible, deadline-bound, or touching shared state, money, auth, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions the specialist would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Verify the real facts (never from memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim cites its proof — file:line, URL of an official source, a measured value, or a posed calculation — found THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSources by domain:\n\n- **Code**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo — every call site, not just the edited file. Hyrum's law: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior (output format, ordering, timing, error text)? Does the new code re-read data written by the old code?\n- **Business / life / admin**: read the actual documents (contracts, statements, notes, Memory files); web-search official sources for external facts (rules, prices, deadlines, opening days); compute the real numbers and dates — count, never estimate.\n- **Physical / creative (3D, drawing, video, print)**: open the actual source files (scene, layers, footage); check the official specs (software docs, platform limits, physical medium constraints); measure, don't eyeball.\n- **People**: who else is affected, who must agree, whose workflow breaks if this changes?\n\nIf the facts can't be reached, label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 universal axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Existing state**: does what already exists (data, commitments, acquired rights) stay valid? Partial overwrite possible?\n2. **Conflicts / concurrency**: two actors or processes on the same resource at once — then what?\n3. **Limits & deadlines**: which cap (budget, size, rate, date, capacity) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security / legal**: who can trigger or abuse this? Compliant with law, terms, licenses?\n5. **Partial failure**: stopped halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, clients, habits, commitments still work?\n7. **Scale & durability**: still fine at 100x the volume, or repeated weekly for a year?\n8. **Recurring cost**: money, time, energy that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it fails silently, how and when do you find out?\n10. **Edge cases**: empty/first-run, oversized, duplicate, offline; holiday, sick day, exception case.\n11. **Checks**: a test or verification that catches the regression BEFORE real impact?\n12. **Reversibility**: one action to undo? Does what got engaged meanwhile (money sent, word given, data written) survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes, expressed in the domain's own units and vocabulary. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks later it failed, and it was THIS decision. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Use THIS domain's high-frequency classics — code: off-by-one, empty/first-run state, stale cache, non-idempotent retry, timezone/encoding, two concurrent writers; admin/life: missed deadline, missing document, misread eligibility rule, transfer delay, closed office; business: nobody pays, no distribution channel; 3D/art/print: wrong units or scale, topology breaks at rig, export color shift, asset license.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → irreversible loss) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (per Step 2) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it: a command, a test, a call, a calculation, a measurement.\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nDécision/changement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recomman